Design for Reuse is a design strategy where used products are returned and put back in the market with a relatively little restoration method (e.g. cleaning). Items under this category should be dismissed by users before they can be considered reusable. This strategy is the circular economy's most advantageous approach in terms of saving time and resources, but  in order for it to work at the best, reusability should create economic advantages. Design for Reuse is sometimes wrongly identified as reuse of the materials of the product. Even if this can be part of the strategy at the very end of the lifespan, the aim of Design for Reuse concern only the reuse of products (or at most the reuse of parts of the products). In this strategt there are various markets, original equipment manufactures can co-operate or compete with independent manufacturers.